/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/google-ace.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("google-ace", [{"t":"Resource hierarchy: organization, folders and projects, and how IAM and organization policies are inherited","body":["Everything you create in Google Cloud sits in a tree called the resource hierarchy. At the top is the organization node, which represents your company and is tied to a Cloud Identity or Google Workspace domain such as example.com. Under it you can create folders, folders can contain other folders, and at the bottom are projects. Every resource, whether a VM, a bucket or a Cloud SQL instance, belongs to exactly one project. The hierarchy matters because it is how you apply access and rules to many resources at once instead of one at a time.","Projects are the basic unit of organization. Each one has its own set of enabled APIs, its own quotas and its own billing link, and it acts as a boundary for most resources. Folders usually mirror how the company works: by department (Finance, Engineering), by environment (Prod, Non-prod), or by team. A new organization is created automatically when a Cloud Identity or Workspace customer first creates a project, and administrators then decide who may create folders and projects.","Identity and Access Management (IAM) allow policies can be attached at the organization, folder, project and many individual resources. A policy is inherited by everything below it, and effective access is the union of all the policies from the resource up to the organization. That makes IAM additive: if a user is Viewer on a folder and Editor on a project inside it, they are Editor on that project. A child cannot remove a grant it inherits from a parent. When you truly need to block a permission regardless of grants, Google Cloud offers IAM deny policies, which are evaluated before allow policies.","Organization policies are a separate mechanism. Instead of saying who can do something, they restrict what can be configured, for example which regions resources may be created in, or whether VMs may have external IP addresses. Organization policies are also set on the organization, a folder or a project and are inherited downward. Depending on the constraint, a lower level can inherit the parent's policy, merge with it or replace it, if administrators allow that.","A practical design is to place broad, stable controls high in the tree (for example, security auditors as viewers at the organization, or a location restriction on an EU folder) and grant narrow, job-specific roles as low as possible, ideally on the project or the resource itself. This keeps least privilege intact and makes it easy to answer who has access to what."],"terms":[["Organization node","The root of the hierarchy, tied to a Cloud Identity or Google Workspace domain."],["Folder","A grouping of projects and other folders that lets you apply IAM and organization policies to all of them."],["Project","The container that every resource belongs to, with its own APIs, quotas, IAM policy and billing link."],["Policy inheritance","IAM and organization policies set on a node apply to every folder, project and resource beneath it."]],"example":"A retailer creates folders named Prod and Non-prod under its organization. The security team is granted Security Reviewer at the organization, the platform team is Editor on the Non-prod folder only, and an organization policy on Prod blocks external IP addresses on VMs. New projects created in either folder get the right access and restrictions automatically.","tip":"IAM allow policies are additive and can't be revoked lower in the tree. If a question says a user still has access after you removed a project-level grant, look for a grant on a folder or the organization above it.","check":[["A user is Viewer on the organization and has no project-level grants. Can they see resources in every project?","Yes. The Viewer grant at the organization is inherited by every folder and project below it."],["What is the difference between an IAM allow policy and an organization policy?","An IAM policy says which principals have which roles. An organization policy restricts how resources may be configured, whatever roles the person has."]]},{"t":"Creating projects: project name, project ID and project number, and enabling APIs","body":["Before you can build anything you need a project. You can create one in the console (the project picker > New project), or from the CLI with `gcloud projects create PROJECT_ID --name=\"Display name\" --folder=FOLDER_ID`. Creating projects requires the Project Creator role on the organization or folder, and the project must be linked to a billing account before you can use most paid services.","Every project has three identifiers, and exam questions like to test the difference. The project name is a friendly display label; you can change it at any time and it doesn't need to be unique. The project ID is a globally unique string of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens that you choose (or accept a generated one) when you create the project. It can never be changed, and it's what gcloud, APIs and many resource names use. The project number is assigned by Google, is also permanent, and shows up in places such as the default Compute Engine service account, `PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com`.","Services in Google Cloud are exposed as APIs, and most of them are disabled in a new project. If you try to use a service whose API is off, you get an error telling you the API has not been used in the project or is disabled. You enable it on the APIs and services page or with `gcloud services enable compute.googleapis.com` (or `run.googleapis.com`, `container.googleapis.com` and so on). `gcloud services list --enabled` shows what's on. Enabling an API costs nothing by itself; you pay for the resources you then create.","Enabling some APIs also creates Google-managed service accounts, called service agents, which let the service act in your project, for example to attach disks or pull images. Don't delete them or remove their roles, or the service will stop working.","When a project is no longer needed, shut it down with `gcloud projects delete PROJECT_ID`. It enters a pending deletion period during which an owner can restore it; after that it is deleted for good and its ID can't be reused."],"terms":[["Project ID","The permanent, globally unique identifier you choose at creation and use in commands and APIs."],["Project number","A permanent numeric identifier Google assigns, used in some service account names."],["Project name","A changeable display label that doesn't need to be unique."],["Service agent","A Google-managed service account that a Google Cloud service uses to act on resources in your project."]],"example":"A developer creates a project with the ID shop-dev-4821 and the name Shop Dev. When she runs `gcloud run deploy`, gcloud offers to enable the Cloud Run API. Later the team renames the project to Shop Development in the console; all scripts keep working because they reference the unchanged project ID.","tip":"If a question says the team wants to change a project ID, the answer is that it can't be done; only the display name can change. An 'API not enabled' error is fixed by enabling the API, not by granting more IAM roles.","check":[["Which project identifier appears in the default Compute Engine service account's email address?","The project number, as in PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com."],["How do you list the APIs enabled in the current project?","Run `gcloud services list --enabled`, or look at APIs and services in the console."]]},{"t":"Organization Policy Service: constraints such as resource locations and disabling service account key creation","body":["The Organization Policy Service gives administrators central guardrails. Where IAM decides who can act, an organization policy decides what configurations are allowed at all. Even a project Owner can't create a resource that an organization policy forbids. This makes organization policies the right answer whenever a requirement says something must never happen, 'even by administrators' or 'even by project owners'."],"locked":true},{"t":"Managing users and groups with Cloud Identity or Google Workspace, and Google Cloud Directory Sync","body":["Google Cloud doesn't have its own user directory inside IAM. People sign in with Google identities: accounts managed in Cloud Identity or Google Workspace for your company's domain, or, less ideally, individual Google accounts such as Gmail addresses. Cloud Identity is an identity-as-a-service product; its free edition gives you managed users and groups for your domain without Workspace apps like Gmail. Workspace includes the same identity features plus the productivity apps."],"locked":true},{"t":"Granting IAM roles to users and groups at the organization, folder and project levels","body":["In IAM, you grant a role to a principal on a resource. A principal can be a Google account, a Google group, a service account, a Cloud Identity or Workspace domain, or special identifiers such as allUsers. A role is a collection of permissions, such as `compute.instances.start`. The combination of principals and a role is a binding, and all bindings on a resource form its allow policy."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Billing accounts: linking projects to billing and the billing IAM roles","body":["A Cloud Billing account defines who pays for Google Cloud usage. It holds the payment profile (a credit card or invoiced billing) and is linked to one or more projects. A project can be linked to only one billing account at a time, but one billing account can pay for many projects. If a project has no active billing account, its paid resources stop: VMs are shut down and billable services become unavailable, and eventually data can be deleted."],"locked":true},{"t":"Budgets, budget alerts and exporting billing data to BigQuery","body":["Cloud Billing gives you several ways to watch spending. Reports in the console show cost trends by project, service, SKU, label and time. For proactive control you create budgets, and for deep analysis you export billing data to BigQuery."],"locked":true},{"t":"Installing and configuring the Google Cloud CLI: gcloud init, named configurations, default project, region and zone","body":["The Google Cloud CLI is the command-line toolset for Google Cloud. It includes `gcloud` for most services, `gsutil` (the older Cloud Storage tool, now largely replaced by `gcloud storage`), and `bq` for BigQuery, and it can install extra components such as `kubectl`. You can install it on Linux, macOS or Windows, and it comes preinstalled in Cloud Shell."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Shell, the Google Cloud console and Cloud Shell Editor","body":["The Google Cloud console is the web interface at the heart of daily work. You use the project picker at the top to choose a project, the navigation menu to reach each product, and the search bar to jump to a service or resource. Many console pages have an Equivalent code or command line link that shows the gcloud command or REST request for what you've configured, which is a great way to learn the CLI."],"locked":true},{"t":"Quotas: viewing usage, understanding quota errors and requesting increases","body":["Quotas protect both you and Google Cloud from unexpected usage. They limit how much of a resource a project can use (allocation quotas, such as the number of CPUs in a region or the number of static IP addresses) or how often you can call an API (rate quotas, such as requests per minute). Separately, system limits are fixed values that can't be raised, such as the maximum size of a single object or disk."],"locked":true},{"t":"Regions, zones and labels: placing resources and organizing them for cost reporting","body":["Google Cloud infrastructure is divided into regions and zones. A region is a specific geographic location, such as us-central1 (Iowa), europe-west1 (Belgium) or asia-northeast1 (Tokyo). Each region contains several zones, such as us-central1-a, us-central1-b and us-central1-c. A zone is an isolated deployment area with its own power, cooling and networking, so a failure in one zone shouldn't affect others in the same region."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing a compute option: Compute Engine, GKE, Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","body":["Google Cloud offers a spectrum of compute services, from full control to fully managed. A large share of exam questions come down to picking the right one, so learn what each is for and what it takes off your plate."],"locked":true},{"t":"Compute Engine instances: machine families, custom machine types, images and Spot VMs","body":["Creating a VM means choosing a zone, a machine type, a boot disk image and networking. From the CLI a basic example is `gcloud compute instances create web-1 --zone=us-central1-a --machine-type=e2-medium --image-family=debian-12 --image-project=debian-cloud`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Compute Engine disks: Persistent Disk and Hyperdisk types, regional disks and local SSD","body":["Compute Engine VMs use block storage for their boot and data disks. The main choices are Persistent Disk, Hyperdisk and local SSD, and they differ in performance, durability and how they're priced."],"locked":true},{"t":"Instance templates and managed instance groups: autoscaling, autohealing and rolling updates","body":["An instance template is a saved VM definition: machine type, boot image, disks, network, metadata such as a startup script, labels and service account. Templates are immutable; to change anything you create a new template. You create one with `gcloud compute instance-templates create web-v2 --machine-type=e2-medium --image-family=debian-12 --image-project=debian-cloud --tags=web --metadata-from-file=startup-script=start.sh`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Google Kubernetes Engine clusters: Autopilot vs Standard, zonal vs regional, and node pools","body":["A GKE cluster has a control plane, which runs the Kubernetes API server, scheduler and controllers, and nodes, which are Compute Engine VMs that run your pods. Google always manages the control plane. What differs between cluster modes is who manages the nodes."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deploying workloads to GKE with kubectl: Deployments, Services and Ingress","body":["Before using kubectl on a GKE cluster, fetch credentials: `gcloud container clusters get-credentials my-cluster --region=us-central1`. This writes an entry to your kubeconfig so that kubectl talks to that cluster's API server using your Google identity. Access inside the cluster is controlled by IAM roles such as Kubernetes Engine Developer, and optionally Kubernetes RBAC."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Run services and Cloud Run functions: deploying containers and event-driven code with Eventarc and Pub/Sub","body":["Cloud Run runs containers without servers or clusters. A container for a Cloud Run service must listen for HTTP requests on the port given in the `PORT` environment variable (8080 by default) and should be stateless, because instances can be started and stopped at any time. You deploy with `gcloud run deploy web --image=us-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/repo/web:1.0 --region=us-central1`, or from source code with `--source .`, which builds the image with Cloud Build."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing a data product: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Firestore, Bigtable and BigQuery","body":["Choosing the right data product is one of the most tested skills. Start by asking: is the data relational or not, is it for transactions (OLTP) or analytics (OLAP), how big will it get, and does it need to span regions?"],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Storage buckets: storage classes, locations, lifecycle rules and Object Versioning","body":["Cloud Storage stores objects (files and their metadata) in buckets. Bucket names are globally unique. You create a bucket with `gcloud storage buckets create gs://my-bucket --location=us-central1 --default-storage-class=STANDARD`, and you choose its location when you create it; you can't change a bucket's location later."],"locked":true},{"t":"VPC networks: auto vs custom mode, subnets, firewall rules, Shared VPC and VPC Network Peering","body":["A Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) network in Google Cloud is global: one VPC can have subnets in many regions, and VMs in different regions of the same VPC can talk over internal IPs without extra setup. Subnets are regional and each has a primary IPv4 range, plus optional secondary ranges used for things like GKE pod and service IPs."],"locked":true},{"t":"Load balancing, Cloud NAT and hybrid connectivity with Cloud VPN and Cloud Interconnect","body":["Cloud Load Balancing is a family of managed load balancers. Pick one by asking three questions: is the traffic HTTP(S) (layer 7) or other TCP/UDP (layer 4), is it from the internet (external) or from inside your network (internal), and is it global or regional?"],"locked":true},{"t":"Infrastructure as code with Terraform and Cloud Marketplace solutions","body":["Infrastructure as code (IaC) means describing your infrastructure in files that are stored in version control, reviewed like code and applied automatically. It gives you repeatable environments, peer review of changes and a history of who changed what. For Google Cloud, the most common tool is Terraform, an open source tool from HashiCorp, used with the Google Cloud provider."],"locked":true},{"t":"Managing Compute Engine VMs: start, stop, resize, SSH with OS Login and IAP TCP forwarding","body":["Day-to-day VM management is mostly a handful of gcloud commands. `gcloud compute instances list` shows VMs with their zones, status and IPs. `gcloud compute instances stop web-1 --zone=us-central1-a` and `start` stop and start a VM; a stopped VM doesn't incur charges for vCPUs and memory, but you still pay for its disks and any reserved static IPs. `suspend` saves memory state to disk and `resume` brings it back. `reset` is a hard restart, like pulling the power cord."],"locked":true},{"t":"Snapshots, snapshot schedules, custom images and image families","body":["Compute Engine has several ways to capture disks and VMs, and each has a different purpose. Knowing which to use is a common exam task."],"locked":true},{"t":"Managing GKE: scaling Deployments, node pool autoscaling, rollouts and rollbacks, and cluster upgrades","body":["Running GKE means managing two layers of capacity: pods and nodes. Keep them straight, because exam distractors mix them up."],"locked":true},{"t":"Managing Cloud Run: revisions, traffic splitting, minimum and maximum instances","body":["Every deployment to a Cloud Run service creates a new immutable revision, named something like web-00007-abc. Revisions keep their image and settings, so you can move traffic back to an earlier one at any time. By default, a new revision receives 100% of traffic as soon as it's healthy."],"locked":true},{"t":"Managing Cloud Storage objects with gcloud storage and moving data with Storage Transfer Service","body":["The `gcloud storage` command group is the current CLI for Cloud Storage and replaces most uses of the older `gsutil`. Buckets and objects are addressed with gs:// URLs. Common commands: `gcloud storage ls gs://my-bucket` lists objects; `gcloud storage cp report.csv gs://my-bucket/reports/` uploads; `gcloud storage cp -r ./site gs://my-bucket` copies a folder; `gcloud storage mv` moves or renames; `gcloud storage rm gs://my-bucket/old/**` deletes; and `gcloud storage rsync` synchronizes a directory with a bucket prefix. Large transfers are parallelized automatically."],"locked":true},{"t":"Operating databases: Cloud SQL backups, high availability and read replicas, and BigQuery dry runs","body":["Cloud SQL handles much of database operations for you, but you choose the protection features. Automated backups run daily in a backup window you choose and are kept for a configurable number of days; you can also take on-demand backups before risky changes. Point-in-time recovery uses transaction logs (binary logs for MySQL, write-ahead logs for PostgreSQL) so you can restore to a specific moment, for example just before a bad DELETE. Restores go to a new or existing instance; they aren't instant failover."],"locked":true},{"t":"Operating networks: expanding subnets, static IP addresses and Cloud DNS","body":["Networks change as workloads grow, and the exam expects you to know which changes can be made in place."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Monitoring: metrics, dashboards, alerting policies, uptime checks and the Ops Agent","body":["Cloud Monitoring collects metrics, which are numeric measurements over time, from Google Cloud services, applications and agents. Many metrics are built in: Compute Engine CPU utilization, network bytes and disk operations; Cloud Run request counts and latencies; Cloud SQL connections; load balancer response codes. A metrics scope lets one project view metrics from several projects, so you can monitor a whole environment from one place."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Logging: Logs Explorer, log-based metrics, log buckets and sinks","body":["Cloud Logging collects log entries from Google Cloud services, audit logs, VMs running the Ops Agent, GKE containers, Cloud Run and your applications (anything written to standard output on serverless platforms, or through client libraries). Each entry has a timestamp, severity, a resource (such as a VM or Cloud Run revision), a log name, and a payload that is either text or structured JSON."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Audit Logs: Admin Activity, Data Access, System Event and Policy Denied logs","body":["Cloud Audit Logs answer the question 'who did what, where and when' for Google Cloud resources. They're written to Cloud Logging and each entry records the principal (user or service account), the method called (such as `v1.compute.instances.delete`), the resource, the time, the caller's IP address and whether the request succeeded. They're essential for security investigations, compliance and troubleshooting."],"locked":true},{"t":"Troubleshooting with Error Reporting, Cloud Trace and Cloud Profiler","body":["Beyond metrics and logs, Google Cloud Observability includes three tools aimed at application problems. Each answers a different question, and exam questions usually hinge on matching the question to the tool."],"locked":true},{"t":"IAM roles: basic, predefined and custom roles, and least privilege","body":["In Google Cloud you never grant permissions directly; you grant roles, which are collections of permissions. A permission has the form service.resource.verb, such as `compute.instances.start` or `storage.objects.get`. Picking the right role is the core of least privilege: give each principal only the access its job requires, at the narrowest scope that works."],"locked":true},{"t":"Viewing and changing IAM allow policies with gcloud, and IAM Conditions","body":["An allow policy is a JSON or YAML document attached to a resource. It contains a list of bindings, each with a role and a list of members, plus an etag and a version. Knowing its shape helps you read command output and exam scenarios."],"locked":true},{"t":"Service accounts: creating them, granting roles, attaching them to resources, and default service accounts","body":["A service account is an identity for a workload, not a person. Applications running on VMs, GKE, Cloud Run or Cloud Run functions use service accounts to call Google APIs. A service account has an email address like `app-sa@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. It is both a principal (it can be granted roles) and a resource (people can be granted roles on it, such as permission to use it)."],"locked":true},{"t":"Service account impersonation, short-lived credentials and avoiding service account keys","body":["A service account key is a long-lived private key file (JSON) that anyone can use to authenticate as the service account, from anywhere, until the key is deleted or disabled. Keys are one of the most common causes of cloud breaches because they get copied into source code, laptops, CI systems and chat messages. Google's guidance is to avoid creating keys whenever another option exists, and many organizations block key creation with the `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation` organization policy."],"locked":true},{"t":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE and for workloads outside Google Cloud","body":["Workloads need identities too, and the goal is to give each one its own short-lived Google credentials without keys. Google Cloud offers two related features, both called Workload Identity Federation, one for GKE pods and one for workloads running elsewhere."],"locked":true},{"t":"Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login for secure administrative access","body":["Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) implements a zero-trust access model: instead of trusting anyone on the right network, it checks who the user is (and optionally their device and context) on every request, and only then lets the request through to the application or VM. It removes the need for VPNs and bastion hosts for many access patterns."],"locked":true},{"t":"Encryption: Google default encryption, Cloud KMS customer-managed keys and customer-supplied keys","body":["All data stored in Google Cloud is encrypted at rest by default, with no action on your part. Google uses envelope encryption: data is encrypted with data encryption keys, which are themselves encrypted with key encryption keys held in Google's internal key management systems. Data in transit between Google data centers and to Google services is also encrypted. For many workloads, this default encryption with Google-owned and Google-managed keys is enough."],"locked":true},{"t":"Secret Manager for passwords, API keys and certificates","body":["Applications need secrets: database passwords, third-party API keys, TLS private keys and tokens. Putting them in source code, container images, plain environment variables in deployment files or configuration buckets is risky, because anyone who can read those places can read the secret, and it's hard to rotate them. Secret Manager is Google Cloud's managed service for storing secrets securely."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud Storage access: uniform bucket-level access, public access prevention and signed URLs","body":["Cloud Storage has two access control systems. IAM grants roles on the project or on a bucket (and, with managed folders, on prefixes), such as Storage Object Viewer, Storage Object Creator or Storage Admin. Access control lists (ACLs) are an older, finer-grained system that can grant access on individual objects. Using both makes it hard to know who can read what."],"locked":true},{"t":"Policy Troubleshooter, IAM recommendations and audit roles for reviewing access","body":["Granting access is only half the job; you also need to understand and review it. Google Cloud's Policy Intelligence tools help you answer 'why can or can't this person do this?', 'who has access to this?', and 'what access is no longer needed?'."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-09-25"});
